# CID syntax cache v3

This isolated source profile adds a bounded, process-local cache only for the
syntax validation of an exact immutable CID string. It does not cache source
bytes, content digests, proof outcomes, current-source membership, or admission.
The cache key includes the allowed codec set and native version/base/hash
constants. The original decoder, canonicality, version, codec and hash guards
run on every miss; public type, lower-case, and allowed-codec guards run on each
call. String subclasses retain the uncached path. The caller's original string
object is returned. Registry mutation is outside this frozen runtime profile.

`cid_parse_cache_validation_v3/controls.json` retains 2,600 reference equalities,
50 changed-source rejections, invalid-type and unsupported-codec controls, and a
separate source-reader spy: twenty matching reads and twenty changed-body reads
perform forty native multihashes even with thirty-nine CID syntax cache hits.
All 64 original content identity, Merkle, source, and freshness tests pass.
The exact source diff and parent hashes are recorded in
`cid_syntax_cache_v3.patch` and `runtime_cid_parse_cache_v3_manifest.json`.

The parent fast-CID encoder is scoped to the existing raw/dag-json allowlist and
normal registered base32 semantics. Its generic fallback does not promise support
for expanding that allowlist at runtime: the parent implementation indexes the
two-codec prefix map before testing the fallback. This limitation is admitted
explicitly; neither frozen parent nor current runtime changes the allowlist.

`results_v9_full_syntax_cache/outcome.json` retains the full XML completion with
exact baseline native state/root identities and 7.793 seconds of preparation
excluding import. Full Pyparsing scanning completes with the same 3,957 symbols,
55,859 edges and state CID in 53.641 seconds, but the producer reaches its original
600-second bound. The experiment does not qualify full Pyparsing preparation.

The actual full-Pyparsing bundle was separately profiled for a 120-second window
in `profile_diagnostic_v3_full_py`. The profile is censored and incurs profiler
overhead: its early costs cannot be interpreted as a complete workload timing.
An attempted non-profiling periodic stack diagnostic exits 139 in the environment
interpreter trampoline; `profile_diagnostic_v4_full_stages/outcome.json` retains
that failure. The original native trials are unchanged. A separately versioned
stage-only diagnostic removes the stack sampler.

All of these are local preparation measurements. No source omission, model
request, token saving, signed trust, or repair-correctness result follows from them.
